{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And we will know them</b> — Their intent was to eliminate wayfaring strangers from among them, according to the words of our Sages (Sanhedrin 109a), for they thought that because of the goodness of their land, which was like the garden of YHWH, many would come there, and they were rejecters of charity. But Lot came to them with his wealth and possessions, or he requested permission from them, or they accepted him for Abraham's honor. And Scripture testifies that this was their intent, as it says (Ezekiel 16:49): \"Behold, this was the iniquity of Sodom your sister: pride of satiety of bread and tranquil ease was hers and her daughters, and the hand of the poor and needy she did not sustain.\" And what it says (Genesis 13:13) — \"evil and sinful against YHWH exceedingly\" — is that they would provoke and rebel in their tranquility and through the oppression of the poor, and this is what it says (Ezekiel 16:50): \"And they were haughty and committed abomination before Me, and I removed them as I saw.\" And according to the opinion of our Sages (see Sanhedrin 109a), they possessed all evil traits, but their decree was sealed on account of that sin — because they did not sustain the hand of the poor and needy — for they were persistent in that sin more than in any other. Moreover, all the nations perform charitable acts with their fellows and with their poor; there was no nation among all the nations like Sodom for cruelty.\n\nKnow that the judgment of Sodom was because of the exalted status of the land of Israel, for it is the portion of the inheritance of HaShem and does not tolerate practitioners of abominations, and when it would vomit out an entire nation on account of their abominations, it first vomited out this people who were worse than all others toward heaven and toward all creatures; the heavens and the earth were appalled at them, and the land was destroyed without remedy forever, because on account of goodness they grew proud. And HaShem saw that it would serve as a sign for wayward children — for Israel who are destined to inherit it — as He warned them: \"Sulfur and salt, a burning of all her land, like the overthrow of Sodom and Gomorrah, Admah and Zeboiim, which YHWH overturned in His anger and wrath\" (Deuteronomy 29:22). For there are nations that are evil and sinful exceedingly, yet He did not do thus to them — but it was all because of the exalted status of this land, for there is the temple of YHWH. And I will yet explain this in the section after the death of two sons of Aaron (Leviticus 18:24), if the One who causes death and the One who restores life will grant me life."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "In the phrase לְמַעֲלַת אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל, לְמַעֲלַת means 'because of the exalted status of,' reading the construct as indicating causation. The closing phrase אִם יְחַיֵּנִי הַמֵּמִית וְהַמְחַיֶּה is a formulaic prayer; the reference to 'the section after the death of two sons of Aaron' points to Leviticus 18, the Torah reading titled אַחֲרֵי מוֹת."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:31:47.808065452Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}